371:F_i^t = 1 + \frac{r_i^t}{1 + \overline{r^t}}
372:\label{eq:fairness}
386:Selection resets a counter to zero and every non-selected client increases its counter by one, so $r_i^t\ge 0$ grows linearly while a client waits. The fairness term of Eq.~(6), $F_i^t = 1 + r_i^t/(1+\overline{r^t})$ with the mean idle count $\overline{r^t}$ of Eq.~(19), is therefore bounded: since $r_i^t\le N\,\overline{r^t}$, it satisfies $1 \le F_i^t < N+1$. The term raises the priority of long-waiting clients; a maximum waiting time is guaranteed by the waiting-time guard of Section~\ref{sec:properties} (Proposition~2), and participation fairness is measured through the JFI of Eq.~(24).
505:\textit{Proof.} (i) $F_i^t\ge 1$ gives $\lambda F_i^t\ge\lambda$, and the clip in Eq.~(8) bounds $a_i^t$. (ii) $r_i^t\le\sum_j r_j^t=N\,\overline{r^t}$, hence $F_i^t=1+r_i^t/(1+\overline{r^t})<1+N$. (iii) and (iv) follow from the clamping and rounding of Eq.~(16) and from the two branches of Eq.~(18). \hfill$\square$
503:\textbf{Proposition 1 (well-posedness).} In every round: (i) the denominator of Eq.~(8) is at least $\lambda>0$ and $a_i^t\in[0,1]$; (ii) $1\le F_i^t<N+1$; (iii) $m_t$ is an integer with $C_{k_{\min}}\le m_t\le C_{k_{\max}}$; (iv) the weights satisfy $S_i^t\ge 0$ and $\sum_{i\in C_k^t}S_i^t=1$.
def test_fairness_range_fixed_population():
    N = 20
    rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
    for _ in range(200):
        r = rng.integers(0, 50, N)
        F = spec.fairness(r, r.mean())
        assert (F >= 1.0).all() and (F < N + 1).all()

